WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.800
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Tim Jackson, who's an ecological economist,

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.370
professor and also author of the book Post
Growth Life After Capitalism.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:11.370
It's so great to have you on.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.370
I appreciate it.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.530
Speaker 3: It's a pleasure to be here, David.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:21.990
Speaker 1: So let's start with something that
to a social Democrat like me is still something

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:28.980
that is in the back of my mind, which is that
we are on a planet that has some maximum capacity

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:35.260
in terms of resources, in terms of population
carrying capacity, etc.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.300
Our economies are based on a little bit of
inflation is good.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.070
And where that inflation comes from is the
expectation that there will be more productivity

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.480
in the future than there is today.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:55.730
And GDP has to continue going up or we consider
that we're in a recession structurally at

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.890
some point that becomes a problem.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:58.890
Right.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.660
Is that a sound place to start the conversation?

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.429
Speaker 3: Yeah, I think it is a sound place
to start the conversation, David, and it's

00:01:05.429 --> 00:01:07.759
definitely where I start as well.

00:01:07.759 --> 00:01:09.680
And it's surprising.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.039
It's surprisingly easy to kind of make that
argument, you know, to to ordinary people

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:14.039
in the streets.

00:01:14.039 --> 00:01:15.720
In a way, we're living on a finite planet.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.100
The economy is supposed to expand forever,
we're told by economists.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.840
Surely at some point in a expanding economy
into finite planet doesn't go so.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.359
So what on earth is going on?

00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:35.679
And and what's surprising, I think, is that
that's a difficult proposition for politicians

00:01:35.679 --> 00:01:41.899
of any color, I would say, to really take
seriously or to know what to do anything about.

00:01:41.899 --> 00:01:48.819
And economists typically, I would have to
say, tend to think that the the economy is

00:01:48.819 --> 00:01:50.219
is about money.

00:01:50.219 --> 00:01:54.270
It's not necessarily about stuff.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:59.239
And so if we're clever enough with our technology,
we can sort of get ourselves off the hook

00:01:59.239 --> 00:02:02.950
and work our way round all of these resource
limits.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.489
And I suppose, you know, that's an argument.

00:02:05.489 --> 00:02:06.489
Speaker 1: Right.

00:02:06.489 --> 00:02:12.060
And so one of the arguments that's frequently
made is while what you're saying is true at

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.739
this point, it's basically theoretical in
the sense that it's very far out.

00:02:16.739 --> 00:02:21.260
And, you know, 100 years ago, nobody could
have predicted exactly the way things are

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:22.260
today.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.740
If we're talking thousands or tens of thousands
of years from now, things will be so structurally

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:30.280
different that this is at minimum going to
act.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.849
In the best case, it will resolve itself in
the worst case, will have the tools with which

00:02:34.849 --> 00:02:38.040
to deal with it by the time it's really a
problem.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.049
But that doesn't really seem satisfying as
an ecological answer.

00:02:42.049 --> 00:02:48.390
Speaker 3: Well, I think I think where it
falls down to is that that might be true for

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:51.400
some kinds of resources that we might run
out of.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:56.069
But actually, we're already running out of
what you could call ecological space.

00:02:56.069 --> 00:02:58.890
You know, we're running out of climate stability.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.900
We're running out of the other species on
the planet that are being decimated because

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.000
of human activity.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.950
We're running out of clean water and clean
air and clean oceans and and that running

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.079
out is actually something that's happening
right now.

00:03:11.079 --> 00:03:13.720
I mean, the evidence on that is alarming.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:20.530
Just simply the climate change evidence, what
was faced by the West Coast in terms of heat

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:25.870
and the East Coast in terms of flooding and
Europe in terms of flooding and Australia

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:27.319
in terms of heat.

00:03:27.319 --> 00:03:30.250
Almost everywhere you look on the planet,
you know, we're seeing these extreme weather

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:37.790
conditions which suggest that, you know, somehow
that far out scenario, that expanding economy

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:41.379
meets the limits of the planet actually isn't
that far out after all.

00:03:41.379 --> 00:03:43.920
That's kind of right here on our doorstep.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:51.249
Speaker 1: One of the issues, I guess, that
economists are confronted with within this

00:03:51.249 --> 00:03:57.159
kind of conversation, within this paradigm
is there are certain aspects of how we think

00:03:57.159 --> 00:03:59.849
about an economy that are kind of taken for
granted.

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:05.780
I mentioned already if GDP isn't growing,
this is a country that is stagnating or in

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.159
a recession in a decline.

00:04:07.159 --> 00:04:10.180
That's one aspect of this debt and deficit.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:15.959
And how that relates to how our country is
doing is is a big piece of this.

00:04:15.959 --> 00:04:23.800
How would those metrics have to be completely
rethought under the umbrella and framework

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:24.800
that you propose?

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.070
Speaker 3: Well, I think I think in a way,
you know, GDP was never a very good metric.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:33.479
I mean, one of the things I do in the book
is go back to the words of of of Robert Kennedy

00:04:33.479 --> 00:04:39.580
speaking in 1968 of fifty three years ago,
where actually he was a prominent politician

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:47.569
prepared to acknowledge the limits of this
measure of the GDP and say, look, it measures

00:04:47.569 --> 00:04:51.080
everything except that which makes life worthwhile,
he said.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:54.050
But it also it misses out the damage to according
to the planet.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:05:00.090
It doesn't include the positive things that
people do for free in their own.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:06.680
It's just not a very good measure of our well-being
and and so so in a sense, you know the answer

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:07.680
to a question.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:11.600
Well, let's start with some better measures
of what really matters and some of those measures,

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:18.020
you know, like our sense of well-being and
our sense of purpose and so on, could be could

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.860
be measures which are very unfamiliar to politicians.

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:22.679
And that's a little bit of a challenge.

00:05:22.679 --> 00:05:27.400
But others of them are really quite simple,
like, you know, not measuring, for example,

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:33.330
how much we are degrading our capital assets
in the economy itself is something that the

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:41.580
GDP is is notorious for for failing to do
and not putting any sort of sense of cost

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:49.889
on the damage that are causing to the environment
and not putting any sort of value on the work

00:05:49.889 --> 00:05:51.610
that people do in their own homes.

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:57.479
All of these things, actually, and there are
very, very good attempts to to improve that

00:05:57.479 --> 00:05:58.909
measure of the GDP.

00:05:58.909 --> 00:06:03.500
But you could almost say at this point in
time, almost any distance towards measuring

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:10.250
something more meaningful than the GDP would
be a step forwards, because it's just not

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:11.800
a very good measure of progress.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.639
It's not a very good measure of human progress
in society.

00:06:14.639 --> 00:06:20.250
Speaker 1: So in terms of some of those things,
the universal basic income movement, which

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:25.159
has different names, proposes itself as a
way to put value to that which is done in

00:06:25.159 --> 00:06:29.820
the home, for example, or that which an economy
may not currently value.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:36.580
Carbon carbon taxes would be a way in which
we might put a monetary value on the degradation

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:40.250
of the environment or the pollution that gets
released, et cetera.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:43.870
Is that thinking too small still or are those
good ideas, good steps?

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:47.650
Speaker 3: No, they are very good ideas in
some, you know, in some ways something like

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:49.330
universal basic income.

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:53.840
And I think when, you know, one of the architects
of the universal basic income, Andre Gorz,

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.580
talking about it first or talking about it
seriously in the 1960s, he wasn't the first

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:00.199
person to talk about it.

00:07:00.199 --> 00:07:06.410
But but what he sort of said was it's a sort
of stealth concept because by changing our

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:10.190
relationship to the way that we earn money
and by changing our relationship to the way

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:15.659
that we work, it enables us to think in massively
creative ways about all the valuable things

00:07:15.659 --> 00:07:22.250
that are happening in society, like, you know,
kind of volunteering in our community and

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:26.229
the free work that happens in the home and
looking after our elderly and looking after

00:07:26.229 --> 00:07:27.229
our kids.

00:07:27.229 --> 00:07:33.389
And it allows us to put a value on those things,
which in the purely capitalist system, in

00:07:33.389 --> 00:07:37.620
the system of the markets, if you like, just
don't have that value on them.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:39.349
So I you know, I'm in favor of those.

00:07:39.349 --> 00:07:46.000
But I still think I mean, to answer your question,
I would say they are tools in a toolbox.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:53.129
And the the difficulty sometimes in implementing
those tools, using those tools, is that the

00:07:53.129 --> 00:07:57.539
architects of the system don't allow them
to be used.

00:07:57.539 --> 00:08:02.099
So they have ecological taxation, for example,
we talked about for decades.

00:08:02.099 --> 00:08:07.300
And yet it's really hard to implement because
of the politics of the growth based economy.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:11.789
Speaker 1: Well, that that's where I was going
to go next, which is when we talk about, you

00:08:11.789 --> 00:08:16.219
know, convincing people, getting people on
board, et cetera.

00:08:16.219 --> 00:08:23.509
It seems that two, four and six year political
terms in in you know, a variety of different

00:08:23.509 --> 00:08:29.199
countries are completely mismatched with the
timeline and priorities that are required

00:08:29.199 --> 00:08:35.560
to deal with these issues where a lot of these
things are longer term projects.

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:42.060
And if your next election is in two years
or four or six, it just seems to be fundamentally

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:46.850
at odds with the timescales that we're talking
about to actually make some of these structural

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:47.850
changes.

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:51.230
Speaker 3: It's it is at odds in many ways.

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:54.420
And I think, you know, that's not the only
thing that's wrong with the political system,

00:08:54.420 --> 00:09:00.100
because the other thing that's wrong is it
becomes in some sense, because of the way

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:06.350
in which political parties become funded,
they end up being sort of apologists for the

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:10.120
status quo, defending vested interests.

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:15.700
And that sometimes stands in the way of them
representing the interests of the poorest

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:19.390
in society, the most vulnerable in society,
including, of course, the other species we

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:20.720
share the planet with.

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:23.170
So we do have to think much more seriously.

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:28.920
I think about, you know, the role of of governance
and the role of power within government.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:33.930
And think about that, as you're suggesting,
with a much longer term view.

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:40.160
And again, there are some interesting initiatives
there that in Hungary there's something called

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:45.510
the ombudsman for future generations, a kind
of representative of people who are coming

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:50.410
way down the line from the future and are
not represented by the democracy of the day.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:57.900
And that's been taken up in Wales, in the
UK, where the future generations act.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:09:59.330
And so this initiative.

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:04.590
If, in fact, to change the way the government
works to bring the future into our decision

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:10.090
making in the present is a really important
part of of thinking about how we address those

00:10:10.090 --> 00:10:11.090
long term issues.

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:17.780
Speaker 1: Certainly part of this would also
be more citizens demanding these things of

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:19.690
elected officials.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:26.920
And there are a lot of sort of bullet points
that are often repeated by those who don't

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:32.690
want to take these issues more seriously about
how such structural change would impact our

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:33.950
day to day lives.

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:38.980
Things about, you know, you might only be
able to have small cars in the United States

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:45.150
or gas might be 20 dollars a gallon, or you
might not be able to use your air conditioning

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:46.210
whenever you want.

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:53.510
A lot of this day to day stuff at the personal
level, what would a paradigm shift, the likes

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:56.170
of which you propose really mean for individuals?

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:58.770
Speaker 3: Yeah, that's a really good question.

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:05.980
I mean, my work over over more than three
decades now has been really working in policy.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:10.680
And it's been about, you know, suggesting
the kinds of economic policies that would

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:12.140
that would address some of these issues.

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:16.950
And in fact, just over 10 years, 12 years
ago, the previous books that that I wrote

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:18.380
was called Prosperity Without Growth.

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:20.500
It was written for policymakers.

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:25.480
It had all of these wonderful ideas, you know,
like ecological tax reform, universal basic

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:29.900
income, different ways of valuing the environment,
different ways of measuring well-being and

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:32.360
all that stuff in it.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:36.280
Almost like a kind of shopping list for what
politicians could do on Monday if they really

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:37.540
cared about this issue.

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:38.540
Right.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:40.660
And it's been fascinating to see that not
happen.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:45.750
And, you know, it doesn't happen for all sorts
of reasons, vested interests, short political

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:52.120
timescales that we talked about, but also
that fear within politics that the electorate

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:54.980
will not stand for this kind of change.

00:11:54.980 --> 00:12:01.200
You know, it's it's a hard, tough place to
be in which our lives will become less comfortable,

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.480
more arduous and so on and so forth.

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:08.650
And so the kind of language of fear that populates
this debate and in a way, what I was trying

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:10.920
to do in Postgres was to say that's a mistake.

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:17.130
You know, the fear that we should have actually
is of an economy that doesn't respect the

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:22.900
environment, that doesn't respect the value
of human labor in our lives, that doesn't

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:31.270
value these things properly at all, and that
is driving us towards a divided world in which

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:37.300
our environmental resources are being degraded
at a rate we can't we can't cope with anymore.

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:39.260
That's where dystopia lies.

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:44.980
And so Postgres is really about looking for
where an escape route from dystopia might

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:45.980
exist.

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:49.410
And I you know, I think that that's led me
across philosophy.

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:50.960
It's led me across psychology.

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:52.490
It's led me across politics.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:58.800
And and and indeed, you know, moral thinking,
thinking about about the ethics of our world.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:04.100
And the thing that leaps out at me, I think
most clearly from that is that this the potential

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:12.280
for for human development, for human progress
outside of this very materialistic, consumer

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:17.410
based, growth driven economy that we've inherited
is huge.

00:13:17.410 --> 00:13:23.040
It's massive because at the end of the day,
you know, the kind of world in which I think

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:28.940
we thrive is one in which our relationships
are strong, in which our community feels secure,

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:35.131
a world in which our work is not just good
in terms of income and keeping us going, but

00:13:35.131 --> 00:13:38.030
is meaningful as it has purpose for us.

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:43.710
And that and that sense, actually, of living
in a world in which there is hope for the

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:49.630
future because we're taking those environmental
limits seriously is a tremendously creative

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:50.630
place.

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:55.410
And I've been fascinated in particular by
ideas from within psychology, which sort of

00:13:55.410 --> 00:14:01.910
suggest that actually somehow materialism
and the comfort of consumerism stand in the

00:14:01.910 --> 00:14:05.710
way of us making real human progress.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:10.710
They stand in the way of us developing skills
because we come to become too lazy.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:16.810
They stand in the way of us developing active,
active lives because we can't become too comfortable

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:20.240
and sit around eating too much and being comfortable
and being comfortable is wonderful.

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:24.390
You know, don't get me wrong, I'm not knocking
being comfortable, but there's a sense in

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:30.600
which being outside of that comfort zone takes
us to our true potential as human beings,

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:35.150
where we learn those skills, where we engage
in activities that give us satisfaction, where

00:14:35.150 --> 00:14:39.810
we feel as though we're embodied in a world
in which we have meaning and purpose and in

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:42.580
which there is hope for the future.

00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:49.660
And to me, therefore, where I came out of
that exploration is really that this post

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:56.440
growth world is a is a richer place to be,
not necessarily in terms of money, definitely

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:59.360
not necessarily in terms of material possessions.

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:05.680
In terms of our capabilities to live truly
fulfilling lives as human beings and to return

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:11.570
to ourselves that sense of hope in the future
Speaker 1: and of course, the challenges in

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:16.740
the midst of consumer culture that bombards
people, it's getting people to even be open

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:20.480
to the idea that that may actually be be richer
and more fulfilling.

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:22.390
Certainly an uphill battle.

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:24.650
The book is, I think,
Speaker 3: the go ahead.

00:15:24.650 --> 00:15:28.370
Yeah, just on that point, David, I mean, I
think that's true.

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:31.600
And yet there are a million places to start
with that.

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:32.600
Right.

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:37.610
You know, how how we how we educate our kids,
how we think about our own progression through

00:15:37.610 --> 00:15:41.640
life, what decisions we make about where we
invest our money and our time.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:43.000
All of those are places to start.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:47.470
Although, you know, there are there are a
lot as you say, there are lots of obstacles

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:49.690
to that, to making those kinds of choices.

00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:54.620
There are also there's a myriad of opportunities
for making them as well.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:55.900
Speaker 1: Well said.

00:15:55.900 --> 00:15:56.900
Well said.

00:15:56.900 --> 00:16:00.220
The book is Post Growth Life After Capitalism.

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:02.670
We've been speaking with Tim Jackson.

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:04.180
I so appreciate your time today.

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:05.180
Speaker 3: It's been a pleasure.

00:16:05.180 --> 00:16:05.310
David.

